trace.branch_0:
Since $n \equiv 2 \pmod{7}$, we have 
\begin{align*}
n + 2 &\equiv 4 \pmod{7}, \\
n + 4 &\equiv 0 \pmod{7}, \\
n + 6 &\equiv 0 \pmod{7}.
\end{align*}
Therefore, 
\begin{align*}
(n + 2)(n + 4)(n + 6) &\equiv 4 \cdot 0 \cdot 0 \equiv \boxed{0} \pmod{7}.
\end{align*}

trace.branch_4:
Since $z^5 = 1,$ $z^3 = 1,$ and $z^2 = 1,$ so
\begin{align*}
z + \frac{1}{z} + z^2 + \frac{1}{z^2} &= z + \frac{1}{z} + 1 + 1 \\
&= z + \frac{1}{z} + 2.
\end{align*}
Then $z + \frac{1}{z} = \boxed{2}.$
